    {
      "id": "claim_e696ef6c0495419fa34c68f0bd5525a8",
      "statement": "The backend's documented dependency direction is API → core → models, one way. API modules (ddpui/api/*_api.py) handle HTTP, permissions, validation and exception-to-HTTP mapping. Core services own business logic and transactions. Models define tables only, and feature exceptions carry no HTTP codes.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/.claude/CLAUDE.md#L5-L42",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6f99bb344d26d41d050a418ffedec4d84998a46c8fadfe46926e4be4e861d231:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/.claude/skills/api-endpoint/SKILL.md#L57-L76",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2ad324044f1058fc582d0f68c6d67d09552a2d637a60ad2ff146378cf839cb8c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_01b49c38b3e84dcbaf72f76ffb12f7a3",
      "statement": "Business logic lives in more than one place. Newer code uses core/{module}/ packages (for example core/kpi, core/orchestrate). Legacy flat core/*functions.py modules and a separate ddpui/services/ package (chart_service, dashboard_service, superset_service and others) also hold service logic. The service-delegation skill names both core/<entity>/ and services/ as owning-service locations.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/.claude/skills/service-delegation/SKILL.md#L24-L28",
